from ../LanguageData import Abbreviations, FirstPerson
from ../meaning/Normalization import SplitCoordinatedSubjects
from ./WordClasses import WordClasses

export anchor Segmentation:
    description:
        Analysis cannot work on a whole block of prose at once. Segmentation
        cuts it into statements: pieces small enough that each says one
        thing. It works from large to small, through three units.

        ```text
        paragraph   lines of prose with no blank line between them
        sentence    ends with a period, question mark or exclamation mark
        statement   one claim, which may be a whole sentence or part of one
        ```

    findingProse:
        Prose is read from the syntax tree, never from the compiled output,
        so nothing is lost to compilation. Consecutive text lines form a
        paragraph; a blank line, a property, a list item or a code block ends
        it. A property whose value sits on its own line counts as prose when
        the value has four or more words; shorter values, like color: blue,
        are structure. Comments are dropped. Code blocks are not prose; they
        are kept as examples.

        A list item is a statement when it has a verb, as in a list of
        rules, and a value when it does not, as in a list of names.

        Before anything else, curly quotes become straight ones and runs of
        whitespace become single spaces.

    sentences:
        A paragraph is cut into sentences after a period, question mark or
        exclamation mark that is followed by a space and then a capital
        letter, a quote, a bracket, a backtick, or a Piton reference. The
        period in an abbreviation from @{Abbreviations} never ends a
        sentence.

    kindsOfSentence:
        Not every sentence states a requirement. Each is one of three kinds.

        ```text
        Does super work on lists?                    a question
        I've found this useful in larger projects.   an aside
        super on a list spreads the parent's items.  a statement
        ```

        A question ends with a question mark. An aside uses a word from
        @{FirstPerson} and no modal that binds, such as must. Questions and
        asides are counted, but analysis draws no facts from them. Every
        other sentence is a statement.

    statements:
        A sentence can make more than one claim, and a statement is one
        claim. A sentence is cut into statements where it joins two clauses,
        each with its own verb, with and, but, or a semicolon.

        ```text
        The button is blue and when disabled it cannot be clicked.

        statement 1   The button is blue
        statement 2   when disabled it cannot be clicked
        ```

        A condition stays with the clause it belongs to, as when disabled does
        here, because a condition cut loose from its clause no longer limits
        anything. When the second clause has no subject of its own, as in the
        button is blue and cannot be clicked, it takes the first clause's.

        Finding where one clause ends and the next begins needs to know which
        words are verbs, so this last cut is made after words are classified,
        as described in @{WordClasses}. A sentence whose subject is a list of
        things is split later still, by @{SplitCoordinatedSubjects}.

    numbering:
        Statements are numbered from 1 within each property, in order, so a
        statement is known by its property path and number: the third
        statement in Button's disabled property is Button.disabled/s3.

    example:
        The running example's prose becomes seven statements.

        ```text
        Button.description/s1   A button is something that can be clicked.
        Button.description/s2   When clicked, its action is invoked.
        Button.description/s3   The button is blue.
        Button.description/s4   It can be enabled or disabled.
        Button.disabled/s1      Its color is dimmed.
        Button.disabled/s2      Its text is dimmed.
        Button.disabled/s3      It cannot be clicked.
        ```
